# tapefuse

tapefuse is a Linux userspace daemon and CLI tool that presents a FUSE
filesystem backed by a real or emulated LTO tape drive using the
[LTFS](https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/ts4300-tape-lib?topic=formats-linear-tape-file-system-ltfs)
(Linear Tape File System) format.  It is composed of two programs:

| Binary  | Role |
|---------|------|
| `ltaped` | Background daemon.  Holds open file handles to tape devices, serves the FUSE mount, and listens for commands on a Unix socket. |
| `ltape`  | Thin CLI client.  Sends JSON-encoded commands to the daemon over the same socket and prints results. |

---

## Features

- **LTFS 2.4.0** index/label format (XML, fully spec-compatible)
- **File-backed simulation** — creates `.p0.dat` / `.p1.dat` partition images on
  any filesystem; no tape drive required for development and testing
- **Real SCSI tape** — reads and writes LTO and MHVTL virtual tape drives on
  Linux using raw `MTIOCTOP` ioctls via `/dev/nst*`
- **FUSE filesystem** — once a tape is loaded and mounted, the contents appear
  as a normal directory; create, read, rename and delete operations are fully
  supported
- **Disk-cached index** — the LTFS index XML is never held only in RAM; it is
  read from and written to `/tmp/ltape/index/<letter>.index` on every metadata
  operation, so the cache is always consistent and survives daemon crashes
- **Explicit commit** — file data is written to tape immediately on `close(2)`,
  but the index must be committed separately; this mirrors how real tape drives
  work and protects against index corruption
- **Physical eject** — `ltape eject` unmounts the FUSE filesystem, waits for
  the FUSE goroutine to exit, and issues the SCSI `MTOFFL` command to
  physically retract the tape before removing the assignment
- **Static binaries** — the CI workflow produces fully statically linked
  `ltaped` and `ltape` with `CGO_ENABLED=0`

---

## Requirements

| Dependency | Purpose |
|------------|---------|
| Linux kernel ≥ 3.x | FUSE (`/dev/fuse`) and tape ioctl support |
| Go ≥ 1.22 | Build toolchain |
| `fuse` kernel module | FUSE mounts (`modprobe fuse`) |
| `mt-st`, `sg3-utils` | Optional: inspecting tape state outside tapefuse |
| MHVTL | Optional: virtual tape library for integration testing |

On real hardware, the tape drive must be accessible as `/dev/nst<N>` (no-rewind
variant).  The file-backed mode works on any OS that supports FUSE (Linux and
macOS with macFUSE), but the SCSI backend is Linux-only.

