## Quick Start

### Using a tape image (no physical drive needed)

```bash
# 1. Start the daemon (in the background or a separate terminal)
./ltaped &

# 2. Assign a device path to a short letter.
#    For file-backed mode, use any non-existent path prefix.
#    ltaped will create <path>.p0.dat and <path>.p1.dat automatically.
./ltape assign /tmp/mytape a

# 3. Initialize (format) the tape image
./ltape init a

# 4. Load the LTFS index from the tape image into the disk cache
./ltape load a

# 5. Mount the filesystem
mkdir -p /mnt/tape
./ltape mount a /mnt/tape

# 6. Use it like any directory
cp myfile.txt /mnt/tape/
ls /mnt/tape/

# 7. Commit: write the updated index back to the tape image
./ltape commit a

# 8. Unmount
./ltape umount a

# 9. Clean up the assignment
./ltape unassign a
```

### Using a real / MHVTL tape drive

```bash
./ltaped &

./ltape assign /dev/nst0 a   # ltaped detects the char device automatically
./ltape init   a             # writes VOL1 + LTFS label + empty index to tape
./ltape load   a             # reads index back into disk cache
mkdir -p /mnt/tape
./ltape mount  a /mnt/tape

cp bigfile.iso /mnt/tape/
./ltape commit a             # flush disk-cached index to tape
./ltape umount a
./ltape eject  a             # MTOFFL ioctl + remove assignment
```

